      "Sn": {
        "type": "control",
        "value": null,
        "control": "Tin is a can-migration analyte only: it enters food by dissolution from tinplate can walls, and fresh, frozen, or otherwise non-canned predatory fish (whole, fillet, loin, or portion) has no tin source. No government sets a tin ML for non-canned fish anywhere in the register; every seafood tin limit is scoped to canned/tinplate product and is excluded by the compiler's canned-gate for this non-canned subcategory. Canned predatory-fish products are certified under the canned-seafood subcategory, where the governing tin ceiling is the canned-food ML (Codex CXS 193 / GSO / India canned foods 250000 ug/kg; Chile canned fish and shellfish 250000 ug/kg). For non-canned product, tin is controlled by screen, not limited: any result above the reporting limit is investigated as evidence of unintended metal contact rather than an inherent property of the fish.",
        "reference_method": "Total Sn by ICP-MS or ICP-OES (e.g. AOAC 985.16 / EN 15764); screen threshold for non-canned product set at the method LOQ, with any quantifiable tin triggering a packaging/contact-material investigation.",
